    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Drawing on ethnographic fieldwork conducted in Ruichong veterinary hospital in Shanghai, this thesis explores the intersections of gender, science, and politics through the lens of pet ownership in urban China. Amidst a booming pet industry and evolving family structures, it examines how urban middle-class women, particularly those born under the One-Child Policy, are redefining kinship by integrating pets into their families.\r\nChapter 1 delves into the scrutiny faced by 'single-child pet moms,' predominantly childless, middle-class women aged 20 to 40, within contemporary biopolitical and economic frameworks. Pets serve both as strategies for managing elder care and as a means to sustain life amidst personal challenges and societal anxieties. The chapter elucidates the roles pets play in contemporary family settings and the reproduction of life, thereby uncovering the nuances of urban womanhood in China. Engaging with Lauren Berlant's concepts of 'lateral agency' and 'slow death,' this chapter complicates existing theories on agency and power.\r\nChapter 2 thoroughly examines the dynamics within the veterinary ophthalmology hospital, interrogating the complex interrelations among pet owners, their pets, and veterinary professionals. The focus is primarily on the analysis of 'failed' medical interventions to uncover the ontological shifts experienced by pets as they transition from being regarded as family members to medical subjects. This analysis explores how medical narratives reconfigure the identities of pets and humans and consequently alter kinship dynamics. The chapter explores the intricate negotiations of trust and ethical considerations, thereby contributing to the discourse on animal agency and veterinary ethics in the urban Chinese context.\r\nCollectively, these chapters illustrate the reciprocal influence between human and animal identities and ontologies, both within familial dynamics and medicalized settings. This interplay reveals a kinship structure that surpasses simplistic dichotomous classifications, advocating for a more intricate understanding of interspecies relationality in the context of urban Chinese socioeconomic realities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
